        "metadata": {
            "kira_final": "Done — `/app/polyglot/main.rs` is a working Rust/C++ polyglot, verified with both toolchains:\n\n- `rustc main.rs -o main && ./main 90` → `4660046610375530309`\n- `g++ -x c++ main.rs -o cmain && ./cmain 90` → same value\n\nHow the trick works:\n\n1. The file starts with `/* /* */` — C++ sees an *unclosed* comment (the first `/*` opens it; the second is nested), so everything down to the matching `*/` after the C++ code is hidden from g++.\n2. Rust doesn't nest comments, so for rustc that first line is just one complete comment (`/* ... */`), and the following C++ code must be hidden from Rust.\n3. That's done by wrapping the entire C++ section in a raw string literal: `auto _ = R\"cpp(` ... `)cpp\";` — Rust sees it as an unused global expression statement, while C++ never parses it because it's inside the big block comment.\n4. The tail has `// */` etc. to close things off cleanly for each parser.\n\nBoth implementations use iterative Fibonacci with f(0)=f(1)=1 (Rust uses `u128`, C++ `unsigned long long`), tested for N=0,1,2,10,90 with identical output.",
